window.MVI_DATA.podcasts=[{"person":"Jim Bianco","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #543 Jim Bianco: Who Solves Inflation The FED or The Market?","episode_date":"2026-07-30","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-543-jim-bianco-who-solves-inflation-the-fed-or-the-market/","variable_id":"UST_30Y_YIELD","topic":"US long-end yields / inflation","direction":"up","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Argues long-term US yields keep rising until inflation is dealt with, either by a hawkish Fed or by the bond market itself.","quote":"So bigger picture, I've been arguing that rates will continue to go higher until Somebody deals with inflation. Now, the deal is somebody dealing with inflation is either A, the Fed panics a little bit and raises rates."},{"person":"Jim Bianco","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #543 Jim Bianco: Who Solves Inflation The FED or The Market?","episode_date":"2026-07-30","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-543-jim-bianco-who-solves-inflation-the-fed-or-the-market/","variable_id":null,"topic":"cost of money / discount rate","direction":"up (and stays higher)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Expects the cost of money to rise and stay higher until the inflation problem is addressed.","quote":"And as it does understand that dynamic, It's going to have to come to grips with this idea that the discount rate, the cost of money is going to go up and it's going to stay higher until we do something about this inflation problem that we have."},{"person":"Jim Bianco","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #543 Jim Bianco: Who Solves Inflation The FED or The Market?","episode_date":"2026-07-30","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-543-jim-bianco-who-solves-inflation-the-fed-or-the-market/","variable_id":"FED_NEXT_ACTION","topic":"Fed policy rate","direction":"hike","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Sees the FOMC majority being able to raise rates, even without the chair, and thinks the chair would probably vote to hike.","quote":"So I could see them raising rates and I could see them doing it without Warsh if they have to do it without Warsh. But at the end of the day, I would argue that he would probably vote to do it"},{"person":"Jim Bianco","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #543 Jim Bianco: Who Solves Inflation The FED or The Market?","episode_date":"2026-07-30","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-543-jim-bianco-who-solves-inflation-the-fed-or-the-market/","variable_id":"BRENT","topic":"oil price risk","direction":"up (risk of price spiral)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Sees the highest-ever risk that oil flips to being the driver of events, citing low inventories and firm demand.","quote":"So I think we're at the highest point ever. of it flipping over to becoming that dependent variable. The inventories are down. We have no cushion anymore. And demand is still up."},{"person":"Harley Bassman","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #550 Harley Bassman: In FED We Trust","episode_date":"2026-09-17","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-550-harley-bassman-in-fed-we-trust/","variable_id":"FED_NEXT_ACTION","topic":"Fed policy rate","direction":"another 25 bp move after about 12 weeks","horizon_text":"about 12 weeks (next move after the current meeting)","summary":"Expects the Fed to wait about 12 weeks and then deliver another 25 bp move; argues 50 bp now would have been better.","quote":"You're going to torture us for another 12 weeks because they're not going in October, clearly. It's before the election. So we're going to wait 12 weeks to go and do 25 more and then think about it."},{"person":"Harley Bassman","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #550 Harley Bassman: In FED We Trust","episode_date":"2026-09-17","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-550-harley-bassman-in-fed-we-trust/","variable_id":null,"topic":"hyperscaler bond credit risk","direction":"stable / low default risk","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Considers AI hyperscaler debt safe even with 50 to 200 bp higher borrowing costs and sees no hyperscaler bankruptcy.","quote":"I think the bonds are probably money good. And if you tack on another 50, 100, 200 basis points, I think it's fine. I just don't see a bankruptcy with the hyperscalers."},{"person":"Luke Gromen","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #542 Luke Gromen: As The Conflict Turns","episode_date":"2026-07-23","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-542-luke-gromen-as-the-conflict-turns/","variable_id":"GOLD","topic":"gold vs oil","direction":"up (relative outperformance vs oil)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Prefers owning gold to trading oil and expects gold to do well and at least keep pace with oil.","quote":"And I look at it and go, you know, for me, it's too hard. Just buy gold. I just look and go, you know what, too hard. I'll just own gold. And I think gold's going to do well over time on less volatility and at least keep up with oil over time in all of this."},{"person":"Luke Gromen","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #542 Luke Gromen: As The Conflict Turns","episode_date":"2026-07-23","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-542-luke-gromen-as-the-conflict-turns/","variable_id":"GOLD","topic":"gold and the war regime","direction":"up","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Says gold has traded down on war escalation but expects that relationship to reverse, with a prolonged war supportive of gold.","quote":"So far, war equals, you know, war hot equals gold down and war off equals gold up. And I think that's about to reverse. I think it may have already reversed."},{"person":"Luke Gromen","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #542 Luke Gromen: As The Conflict Turns","episode_date":"2026-07-23","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-542-luke-gromen-as-the-conflict-turns/","variable_id":null,"topic":"yield curve control / Western government bond markets","direction":"yield-curve control likely","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Expects bond-market stress to force the West into implicit or explicit yield curve control.","quote":"I think the ultimate outcome is that it's going to force the West into either some form of implicit or explicit yield curve control to control their bond markets. Because when you look at what the West is doing, not only is war always inflationary, it's always inflationary. Always."},{"person":"Richard Clarida","role":"guest","show":"Macro Hive","episode_title":"Ep. 368: Richard Clarida on the Warsh Fed, the AI Capex Boom, and Global Economic Fragmentation","episode_date":"2026-07-24","episode_url":"https://macrohive.libsyn.com/ep-368-richard-clarida-on-the-warsh-fed-the-ai-capex-boom-and-global-economic-fragmentation","variable_id":"FED_RATE_LEVEL","topic":"Fed policy rate","direction":"flat this year; possible 1-2 cuts next year","horizon_text":"this year; next year (data dependent)","summary":"States PIMCO's baseline: no Fed hike and no cut this year, with room to cut once or twice next year if inflation returns toward 2%.","quote":"PIMCO's baseline view is that we will not get a hike out of the Warsh Fed this year. We don't think we're going to get a cut either. And we think that if the inflation data begins to return back towards 2% next year, that that will give the committee room to cut rates perhaps once or twice"},{"person":"Richard Clarida","role":"guest","show":"Macro Hive","episode_title":"Ep. 368: Richard Clarida on the Warsh Fed, the AI Capex Boom, and Global Economic Fragmentation","episode_date":"2026-07-24","episode_url":"https://macrohive.libsyn.com/ep-368-richard-clarida-on-the-warsh-fed-the-ai-capex-boom-and-global-economic-fragmentation","variable_id":null,"topic":"real interest rates / AI productivity","direction":"up (conditional on a productivity boom)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Says a large AI-driven productivity boom would imply higher real interest rates, while the size of the effect is uncertain.","quote":"And so I think, put it this way, I don't know the effect of AI on interest rates. But if you tell me AI is going to boost productivity growth from 2% a year to 4% a year, real interest rates are going to be higher."},{"person":"Marvin Barth","role":"guest","show":"The Grant Williams Podcast","episode_title":"The Grant Williams Podcast: Marvin Barth","episode_date":"2026-08-13","episode_url":"https://permalink.castos.com/podcast/19087/episode/2568679","variable_id":null,"topic":"US long-end Treasury yields / term premium","direction":"stress at some point, not imminent","horizon_text":"'at some point', not 'particularly soon'","summary":"Sees the long end of the US curve as a looming problem but not an imminent one.","quote":"So that does suggest that we're going to run into a problem at some point, and maybe that's sooner rather than later. I don't get the sense that it's going to be particularly soon because we always run into the same problem."},{"person":"Daniel Lacalle","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #547 Daniel Lacalle: The Future of Reserve Currency","episode_date":"2026-08-27","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-547-daniel-lacalle-the-future-of-reserve-currency/","variable_id":null,"topic":"US dollar reserve status","direction":"dollar stays dominant fiat reserve currency (conditional)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Expects the dollar to remain the world's main fiat reserve currency, provided US fiscal imbalances are reined in.","quote":"and that if the U.S. government really rains on its massive imbalances, the U.S. dollar will continue to be the world fiat reserve currency because there is no alternative in the fiat world, i.e. the dollar is not the world reserve currency in the fiat world because it's better, but because the alternatives are worse."},{"person":"Viktor Shvets","role":"guest","show":"MacroVoices","episode_title":"MacroVoices #544 Viktor Shvets: How Markets Survive Disruption","episode_date":"2026-08-06","episode_url":"https://macrovoices.podbean.com/e/macrovoices-544-viktor-shvets-how-markets-survive-disruption/","variable_id":"GOLD","topic":"gold as safe haven","direction":"up / outperform USD and Treasuries as haven","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Thinks the current preference for USD and Treasuries as a haven is temporary and that gold remains the ultimate safe asset.","quote":"But I think it's temporary because at the end of the day, to me, gold remains the ultimate safety assets in a case of an absolute destruction, so to speak, where monetary systems melt down, where you have a situation where reset social and political systems, where you have multiple wars. I still think gold is the answer."},{"person":"Brent Johnson","role":"guest","show":"The Grant Williams Podcast","episode_title":"The Grant Williams Podcast: Brent Johnson","episode_date":"2026-07-02","episode_url":"https://permalink.castos.com/podcast/19087/episode/2513767","variable_id":"DXY","topic":"US dollar","direction":"up over time (can be weak short term)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Argues the dollar can be weak in the short term but eventually recovers as global dollar-debt demand reasserts.","quote":"And that is why the dollar eventually, even though it can get weak in the short term, eventually comes back because eventually that dollar demand kicks in."},{"person":"Brent Johnson","role":"guest","show":"The Grant Williams Podcast","episode_title":"The Grant Williams Podcast: Brent Johnson","episode_date":"2026-07-02","episode_url":"https://permalink.castos.com/podcast/19087/episode/2513767","variable_id":"GOLD","topic":"gold in reserve management","direction":"up (rising reserve role)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Expects gold to play an increasing role in central-bank reserve management.","quote":"It does diversify you away from the US system. So I would expect gold to continue to play an increasing role in reserve management."},{"person":"Rick Rule","role":"guest","show":"The Grant Williams Podcast","episode_title":"The Grant Williams Podcast: Rick Rule","episode_date":"2026-06-12","episode_url":"https://permalink.castos.com/podcast/19087/episode/2489845","variable_id":null,"topic":"US dollar purchasing power / inflation","direction":"down (loses 75%)","horizon_text":"next 10 years","summary":"Expects the dollar to lose about 75% of its purchasing power over ten years via 1970s-style inflation.","quote":"I believe specifically that the U.S. dollar over the next 10 years loses 75% of its purchasing power, which is to say that $120 trillion in off-balance sheet liability becomes a nominal 120, but a real 40."},{"person":"Rick Rule","role":"guest","show":"The Grant Williams Podcast","episode_title":"The Grant Williams Podcast: Rick Rule","episode_date":"2026-06-12","episode_url":"https://permalink.castos.com/podcast/19087/episode/2489845","variable_id":"GOLD","topic":"precious metals / resource commodities","direction":"holds real value (no price level given)","horizon_text":null,"summary":"Says precious metals and resource commodities preserve real purchasing power in a period of currency devaluation.","quote":"I'm not suggesting, by the way, that the gold price goes up 25-fold. What I am suggesting is that precious metals in particular, but also other resource commodities, maintain their real as opposed to their nominal purchasing power, unlike very few other assets."}];
